content.text:
You're right, and my reason was weak. Drizzle can build the Postgres enum straight from the TypeScript list, so the six names would still be written in only one place:

```ts
// src/lib/categories.ts
export const CATEGORIES = ["paid", "promise_to_pay", "dispute", "resend_invoice", "out_of_office", "needs_human"] as const;
// src/db/schema.ts
export const categoryEnum = pgEnum("category", CATEGORIES);
```

The only real cost is the one I mentioned: changing the list needs a migration. Adding a value is easy; renaming or removing one is awkward. That doesn't matter here, because the spec fixes the six categories.

An enum also makes the database refuse any value that isn't one of the six. Given that a wrong `paid` is our costliest mistake, that extra guard is worth having. One enum covers all four category columns (`model_category`, `final_category`, `from_category`, `to_category`), because POLICY.md uses the same six names for both the parent categories and the v0 categories.

To do this I'll create a small `src/lib/categories.ts` now, ahead of step 3. In step 3 I'll add the rank mapping to it. While I'm at it, I'll make `source` and `confidence` enums too, built the same way.

